            title: 'The Office in a Pocket: How Remote Work Changed the World of Jobs',
            text: 'Twenty years ago, working from home was a rare privilege. In 2005, fewer than one in twenty employees in Britain worked from home on a regular basis, and most managers believed that staff could not be trusted to work without supervision. Laptops were heavy, internet connections were slow, and the idea of a meeting held by video call seemed strange and futuristic.\n\nThe turning point came when technology quietly caught up with the dream. Cheap broadband, smartphones and cloud storage meant that an office worker in Leeds could open the same files as a colleague in London at exactly the same moment. Then, in 2020, the pandemic forced millions of people to work from their kitchen tables almost overnight. Companies that had resisted change for a decade discovered within weeks that their businesses could survive — and sometimes even improve.\n\nSarah Lennox, a 34-year-old graphic designer from Leeds, is typical of the new remote workforce. She has not visited her company office for more than two years, yet she produces more work than ever before. "I save almost two hours a day because I no longer travel into the city," she says. "I use that time for my family and for exercise, so I start work feeling fresher." Her employer, a marketing firm with 120 staff, closed its expensive city-centre office in 2022 and now rents a small meeting space only when teams need to gather.\n\nSurveys support Sarah\'s experience. A 2023 report by the Workplace Institute found that 68 per cent of remote workers said they were more productive at home, mainly because they faced fewer interruptions. However, the same report warned that 41 per cent of younger employees felt lonely and missed the social side of office life. Loneliness was a particular problem for staff who lived alone in small flats.\n\nRemote work has also changed geography. When your office is a laptop, you can live almost anywhere. Small towns and villages, which once lost their young people to the cities, have seen families move in, buying houses and using local shops. Meanwhile, city-centre cafes and sandwich bars that depended on office workers have lost customers, and some have closed.\n\nNot every job can follow this pattern, of course. Nurses, builders, bus drivers and chefs cannot do their work through a screen, and some critics say society is dividing into two groups: those who enjoy freedom and flexibility, and those who must still travel to a workplace every day. Others warn that companies may start hiring cheaper workers in foreign countries if distance no longer matters.\n\nWhat seems certain is that the change cannot be reversed. Most surveys show that a majority of office workers would now refuse a job that demanded five days a week in an office. The office has not died, but it has become a place people visit for meetings and friendship — not a place where they must sit from nine until five.',
