        title: 'A New Job at Brightpath Logistics',
        script: 'Woman: Good morning, Brightpath Logistics. How can I help you?\nMan: Good morning. My name is Daniel Carter. I am starting work here on Monday, and I have a few questions before my first day.\nWoman: Of course, Daniel. Welcome to the company. I am Priya from Human Resources. What would you like to know?\nMan: First, what time should I arrive on Monday?\nWoman: Please come to reception at half past eight. Your training starts at nine, but we need about thirty minutes to take your photograph and prepare your security pass.\nMan: I see. And where exactly is the office? I know it is near the station.\nWoman: Yes, we are on Mill Road, number forty-two. It is a five-minute walk from Central Station. If you come by car, there is a staff car park behind the building, but you need a permit, so please use public transport on your first day.\nMan: That is fine. I will take the train. What should I bring with me?\nWoman: Please bring your passport and your bank details, so we can set up your salary payments. Also bring the contract that we sent you, signed on the last page.\nMan: I have already signed it. Now, about the job itself — will I be working in the warehouse or in the office?\nWoman: You will spend the first two weeks in the office, learning our computer systems. After that, you will work three days a week in the office and two days in the warehouse, helping the dispatch team.\nMan: And who will be my manager?\nWoman: Your line manager is Mr Tom Ellery. He is in charge of the dispatch team, and he will meet you after your training session on Monday morning.\nMan: Good. What about lunch? Is there a canteen?\nWoman: Yes, there is a staff canteen on the ground floor. It is open from twelve until two, and a hot meal costs four pounds fifty. There is also a small kitchen if you prefer to bring your own food.\nMan: That sounds reasonable. One more question — what is the dress code? Should I wear a suit?\nWoman: No, a suit is not necessary. We ask staff to dress smartly — a shirt and trousers are fine. But please note that in the warehouse you must wear safety shoes, and the company will provide those free of charge.\nMan: Excellent. And when will I receive my first salary?\nWoman: Salaries are paid on the last Friday of every month, directly into your bank account. Because you are starting in the middle of the month, your first payment will cover six weeks.\nMan: That is very helpful. Finally, is there anything I should do before Monday?\nWoman: Yes — please complete the short online safety course. It takes about forty minutes, and I will email you the link this afternoon. You cannot enter the warehouse until you have finished it.\nMan: No problem at all. Oh, one more thing — is there a probationary period?\nWoman: Yes, the first three months are a trial period for all new staff. During that time, Mr Ellery will meet you every two weeks to discuss your progress, and you will receive extra training if you need it. Almost everyone passes without difficulty.\nMan: And if I complete it successfully?\nWoman: Then your contract becomes permanent, and your salary is reviewed. There is also a staff discount card for our partner shops after six months, and twenty-five days of paid holiday each year, plus public holidays.\nMan: That sounds very fair. What happens if I am ill one day?\nWoman: Simply telephone the office before eight o\'clock and speak to your manager. For the first two days you do not need a doctor\'s note, but for longer illnesses you do.\nMan: Understood. Thank you very much for your help, Priya. I feel much more prepared now.\nWoman: You are welcome, Daniel. We look forward to seeing you on Monday. Goodbye.',
