          sample: 'The bar chart compares the proportion of workers in farming, manufacturing and services in a particular country in three years: 1990, 2005 and 2020.\n\nOverall, the service sector employed the largest share of workers in every year and grew steadily, whereas employment in both farming and manufacturing fell significantly over the period.\n\nIn 1990, services accounted for half of all workers, at 50 per cent. Manufacturing employed just over a third, at 35 per cent, while farming was the smallest sector, with only 15 per cent of the workforce. By 2005, the pattern had begun to change. The service sector had risen to 60 per cent, but manufacturing had dropped to 25 per cent and farming to 10 per cent.\n\nBy 2020, the gap between the sectors had become dramatic. Seven out of ten workers were employed in services. In contrast, manufacturing provided jobs for only 20 per cent of workers, and farming for just 10 per cent, with the farming figure halving between 1990 and 2020.\n\nIn summary, the country moved away from producing food and goods towards providing services during these thirty years.'
