      writing: {
        task1: {
          title: 'Recorded crimes in a city, 2010–2020',
          text: 'The table below shows the number of three types of crime recorded in one city in 2010, 2015 and 2020.\n\nBurglary: 2,400 (2010), 1,900 (2015), 1,350 (2020). Car theft: 1,800 (2010), 1,500 (2015), 1,100 (2020). Shoplifting: 900 (2010), 1,400 (2015), 2,100 (2020).\n\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.',
          sample: 'The table compares the number of recorded burglaries, car thefts and shoplifting offences in one city in 2010, 2015 and 2020.\n\nOverall, burglary and car theft became much less common over the period, falling steadily in both cases. In contrast, shoplifting increased dramatically and was the most frequent of the three crimes by 2020.\n\nIn 2010, burglary was the most common crime in the city, with 2,400 recorded cases. Car theft was also widespread, at 1,800 cases, while shoplifting was the least frequent offence, with only 900 cases. Over the next five years, the figures moved closer together: burglary fell to 1,900 and car theft to 1,500, whereas shoplifting rose to 1,400.\n\nBy 2020, the picture had changed completely. Shoplifting had more than doubled since 2010, reaching 2,100 cases and overtaking the other two crimes. Meanwhile, burglary had dropped to 1,350 cases, and car theft had fallen to 1,100, less than two-thirds of its 2010 level.\n\nIn summary, the city became safer in terms of serious property crime, but theft from shops grew into its biggest problem.'
        },
        task2: {
          title: 'Prison or rehabilitation?',
          text: 'Some people believe that the best way to reduce crime is to send criminals to prison for long sentences. Others argue that education and rehabilitation programmes are more effective.\n\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.\n\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\n\nWrite at least 250 words.',
          sample: 'How societies should deal with criminals is a question that provokes strong opinions. Some people insist that long prison sentences are the most effective answer, while others claim that educating and rehabilitating offenders works better. I believe that both approaches are necessary, but for different types of crime.\n\nThose who favour long sentences argue that prison performs two essential jobs. Firstly, it protects the public: a violent criminal who is locked away cannot harm anyone outside. Secondly, the fear of a long sentence may deter others from committing similar offences. When a court gives a serious punishment for a serious crime, victims also feel that justice has been done, which maintains public confidence in the law.\n\nOn the other hand, supporters of rehabilitation point to some uncomfortable facts about prison. A large proportion of prisoners — in many countries, more than half — commit new crimes after they are released. Prison can mix first-time offenders with experienced criminals, and a criminal record makes it almost impossible to find honest work afterwards. Education programmes, job training and help with addiction, by contrast, attack the reasons why people offended in the first place. A young shoplifter who leaves a programme with a qualification and a job is far less likely to steal again than one who leaves a cell with nothing.\n\nIn my opinion, the sensible policy is a combination. Dangerous and violent criminals must receive long sentences, because public safety comes first. However, for the majority of offenders, who commit minor, non-violent crimes, rehabilitation offers a cheaper and more successful long-term solution.\n\nIn conclusion, prison will always be needed for the most serious offences, but if the real goal is less crime in the future, helping offenders to change is at least as important as punishing them.'
        }
      },
