          text: 'The bar chart below shows the main source of news for adults in one country in 2005 and 2023.\n\nIn 2005: television 62 per cent, newspapers 24 per cent, radio 9 per cent, internet and social media 5 per cent. In 2023: television 31 per cent, newspapers 6 per cent, radio 8 per cent, internet and social media 55 per cent.\n\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.\n\nWrite at least 150 words.',
          sample: 'The bar chart compares the main sources of news for adults in one country in 2005 and 2023.\n\nOverall, television was by far the most popular news source in 2005, but by 2023 the internet and social media had taken its place. Newspapers suffered the sharpest fall in popularity, while radio remained a minor source in both years.\n\nIn 2005, almost two-thirds of adults, 62 per cent, got their news mainly from television. Newspapers were the second most important source, used by 24 per cent, whereas radio and online sources were far behind, at 9 per cent and only 5 per cent respectively.\n\nBy 2023, the picture had changed dramatically. Internet and social media had become the main source for 55 per cent of adults — eleven times the 2005 figure. Television had fallen by half, to 31 per cent, and newspapers had collapsed to just 6 per cent. Radio was the most stable source, slipping only slightly, from 9 per cent to 8 per cent.\n\nIn summary, news consumption in this country moved decisively from traditional media to online platforms over the eighteen-year period.'
