        task2: {
          title: 'Advertising and unnecessary buying',
          text: 'Many people complain that advertising encourages us to buy things we do not really need. Others say that advertising is essential for a healthy economy.\n\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.\n\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\n\nWrite at least 250 words.',
          sample: 'Advertising is impossible to escape: it fills our television breaks, our streets and our phones. Many people blame it for persuading us to waste money on unnecessary products, while others defend it as the engine of the modern economy. In my opinion, both views contain some truth.\n\nCritics of advertising make a powerful case. Modern adverts rarely provide useful facts about a product; instead, they attack our emotions, suggesting that a new phone, car or pair of shoes will make us happier, younger or more popular. The fashion industry is a clear example: last year\'s clothes are presented as embarrassing so that consumers replace perfectly good items. The results are visible in household debt, in wardrobes full of unworn clothes, and in mountains of waste.\n\nNevertheless, it would be wrong to see advertising as purely harmful. In a market economy, businesses must tell customers that their products exist, and advertising allows new and small companies to compete with famous brands. It also pays for much of the media we enjoy for free: newspapers, websites, radio stations and television channels all depend on advertising income. Without it, consumers would have to pay directly for almost everything they watch and read. Finally, advertising creates thousands of jobs in design, film and marketing.\n\nMy own view is that the answer lies in regulation rather than abolition. Advertising should be honest, it should not target young children, and it should not make claims that a product cannot support. With sensible rules, society can keep the economic benefits of advertising while limiting its worst effects.\n\nIn conclusion, advertising does encourage unnecessary buying, but it also informs consumers and funds our media. The task is to control it, not to kill it.'
        }
